@@ -92,7 +92,7 @@
 
 ### Changed
 
-- Align the library's diagnostic strings with Guava's `ListenableFuture` rendering philosophy. Future views now render Guava's identity-bearing envelope: the internal execution and adopted-member futures name their task or member and phase hint while pending (the race-safe `pendingToString()` hook), `ImmediateResult.asFuture()` renders `ImmediateResult.AsFuture@identityHash[status=...]` with the always-terminal success or failure branch instead of the Guava delegate's full value, a task future renders `Task@identityHash[status=..., task='name']` without the clock-dependent remaining budget and redoes the terminal branch when a completion lands mid-render, and the read-only completion, group-values, and starvation-report views render their own `ReadOnlyView@identityHash[status=...]` envelope instead of their sink's. A success value renders as `SimpleClassName@identityHash` ("null" when null, "this future" for self-reference) in `ImmediateResult` and `TaskTermination`; a failure renders as its type only in `ImmediateResult`, `FailureCause`, `Task`, and batch reports, so logging a result can neither run user rendering code nor copy a throwable message that may carry credentials. Aggregate diagnostics print counts instead of contents: `GroupValues` prints its member count, `ExecutorWaitReport` its component count (a wait cycle component its member, cycle, and witness counts), `WorkloadFitReport` its entry count (an entry measurement its unit count), and `ThreadPoolStarvationReport` its evidence and unsupported-executor counts without the sampling failure message, with details still available through the existing accessors (`ThreadPoolStarvationReport.failureMessage()` keeps the message for explicit inspection). `ScopedCallable` renders the user task code by identity. These formats remain diagnostic and are not a parsing contract.
